  "theorem_name": "CompleteTargetPeriodCharacterCancellationAndPointwiseNoGo",
  "declared_proposition": "For an odd prime l, let M_a(u,v)=1_{uv!=a} on the unit group. The complete moving-target sum satisfies sum_(a mod l) M_a=(l-1)J, so it annihilates every nonconstant character exactly. For any H consecutive targets, complete residue periods vanish on that space and the normalized remainder has operator norm at most r/H<l/H, where r=H mod l. In contrast, each fixed nonzero M_a restricts to minus a permutation and has nonconstant operator norm exactly one.",
  "mathematical_argument": "For fixed units u,v there is exactly one nonzero residue a=uv. Among all l targets, M_a(u,v) is one zero and l-1 ones, proving the rank-one complete-period identity. On the zero-sum space J vanishes. Splitting an H-window into complete periods and a remainder of r targets, then using the triangle inequality and ||M_a||=1 on the nonconstant space, gives r/H. For one nonzero target, M_a=J-P_a=-P_a on that space, and P_a is a permutation isometry.",
